/Contents (Not bipartite because there are 3 vertices here. And all of them are connected to each other. So, if I try to divide them into two separate sets, one will have 2 vertices and the other will have one Vertice. The two vertices are in a triangle, but in a set to be bipartite, vertices in the same set cannot be connected to each other. so, a triangle cannot be bipartite.)
